      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/9ec9b64e62efa9435f5d00126164a01628c86ab16a49f52b478e81a83e5b7d2a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(-1),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,0].$ Notice that $x=-1$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/2c01b2337e4ca3a872e9f72ef1b52f0190096203ba2f0a4896cc11a028578d66.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,0)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-(-1)}{0-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
